ROOT CAUSE (mechanism, measured -- not inferred)
  XMN5's VSS port sits at y = -190.8923, which is fractional grid row 40.545 at the
  baseline pitch 0.72um (origin y0 = -220.0849). The router landed on row 41 and
  dropped the met3->met2 via one row DOWN at row 40 (y = -191.2849):
     landing-stub bottom edge = port - 0.105          = -190.9973
     via met3 pad top edge    = -191.2849 + 0.165     = -191.1199
     gap                      = 0.1227um  <  0.14um   -> met2.2 FAIL
  The 0.21um connecting wire spans only x[-401.572,-401.362], leaving
  x[-401.630,-401.572] open as a 0.058 x 0.123um notch. It is a SAME-NET notch
  between two pieces of VSS -- electrically harmless, but a hard DRC failure.

KEEPOUT CHECK (done FIRST, before any knob)
  Only XC0 declares a keepout in primitives/manifest.json (keepout_um = 1.2).
  XC0 occupies x[-439.09,-402.81] y[-175.91,-135.37]; the violation is ~15um away.
  No keepout was breached, so this is not a placement-contract violation.

CLASSIFICATION: routing-fixable (quantized near-miss), NOT router-defect.
  The port is fixed by the macro, but the VIA ROW is a routing decision, and moving
  the grid moves it. Confirmed by the fix succeeding.

