2. WHY ONLY ONE KNOB COULD WORK -- measured, and worth carrying forward
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
route_nets.py's CongestionMap.penalty() short-circuits on a net's own metal
("Its own metal is free -- a net rejoining its own tree is the normal case"), so
shared_cell_penalty, via_pad_penalty and proximity_* are STRUCTURALLY BLIND to a
same-net notch. Verified empirically at baseline pitch -- every one of these left
the violation at exactly 1 gap region, unchanged:
    --via-cost 5 | --via-cost 20 | --via-pad-penalty 200 | --proximity-radius 3
    --proximity-weight 2.0 | --wrong-direction-penalty 3.0 | --over-macro-cost 0.8
    --ripup-iters 12 | --shared-cell-penalty 1000
Only --track-multiplier (re-quantization) moved the geometry. Screened sweep:
    tm 1.02 clean | 1.04 clean | 1.05 clean | 1.06 clean | 1.08 DIRTY(1)
    tm 1.10 clean | 1.12 clean | 1.15 DIRTY(2) | 1.20 clean | 1.25 DIRTY(1)
    tm 1.30 DIRTY(1) | 1.50 DIRTY(2)
Screening used a dilate/erode geometric spacing check that exactly reproduced
Magic's single baseline violation before being trusted; Magic remained the authority
and was run on the chosen candidate.

